  <subsection title="Multiply a Polynomial by a Monomial">
    <p>In Distributive Property you learned to use the Distributive Property to simplify expressions such as $$2(x - 3).$$ You multiplied both terms in the parentheses, $$x\ \text{and}\ 3,$$ by $$2,$$ to get $$2x - 6.$$ With this chapter's new vocabulary, you can say you were multiplying a binomial, $$x - 3,$$ by a monomial, $$2.$$ Multiplying a binomial by a monomial is nothing new for you!</p>
    <example number="10.28">
      <problem>
        <p>Multiply: $$3\left( {x + 7} \right).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$3\left( {x + 7} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$3 \cdot x + 3 \cdot 7$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$3x + 21$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.55">
      <p>Multiply: $$6{\left( {x + 8} \right).}$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.56">
      <p>Multiply: $$2{({y + 12}).}$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="10.29">
      <problem>
        <p>Multiply: $$x\left( {x - 8} \right).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.57">
      <p>Multiply: $$y{({y - 9})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.58">
      <p>Multiply: $$p{({p - 13})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="10.30">
      <problem>
        <p>Multiply: $$10x(4x + y).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.59">
      <p>Multiply: $$8x(x + 3y).$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.60">
      <p>Multiply: $$3r(6r + s).$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>Multiplying a monomial by a trinomial works in much the same way.</p>
    <example number="10.31">
      <problem>
        <p>Multiply: $$-2x{({5x^{2} + 7x - 3})}.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$-2x{({5x^{2} + 7x - 3})}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$-2x \cdot 5x^{2} + (-2x) \cdot 7x - (-2x) \cdot 3$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$-10x^{3} - 14x^{2} + 6x$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.61">
      <p>Multiply: $$-4y{({8y^{2} + 5y - 9})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.62">
      <p>Multiply: $$-6x{({9x^{2} + x - 1})}.$$</p>
    </note>
    <example number="10.32">
      <problem>
        <p>Multiply: $$4y^{3}(y^{2} - 8y + 1).$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$4y^{3}(y^{2} - 8y + 1)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$4y^{3} \cdot y^{2} - 4y^{3} \cdot 8y + 4y^{3} \cdot 1$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$4y^{5} - 32y^{4} + 4y^{3}$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.63">
      <p>Multiply: $$3x^{2}(4x^{2} - 3x + 9).$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.64">
      <p>Multiply: $$8y^{2}(3y^{2} - 2y - 4).$$</p>
    </note>
    <p>Now we will have the monomial as the second factor.</p>
    <example number="10.33">
      <problem>
        <p>Multiply: $$\left( {x + 3} \right)p.$$</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\left( {x + 3} \right)p$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Distribute.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$x \cdot p + 3 \cdot p$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Simplify.</cell>
              <cell>$$xp + 3p$$</cell>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.65">
      <p>Multiply: $$\left( {x + 8} \right)p.$$</p>
    </note>
    <note type="Try It" number="10.66">
      <p>Multiply: $$\left( {a + 4} \right)p.$$</p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection title="Multiply a Binomial by a Binomial">
    <p>Just like there are different ways to represent multiplication of numbers, there are several methods that can be used to multiply a binomial times a binomial.</p>
    <subsection title="Using the Distributive Property">
      <p>We will start by using the Distributive Property. Look again at Example 10.33.</p>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell />
            <cell />
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>We distributed the $$p$$ to get</cell>
            <cell />
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>What if we have $$(x + 7)$$ instead of $$p$$?</cell>
            <cell />
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Distribute $$(x + 7)$$.</cell>
            <cell />
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Distribute again.</cell>
            <cell>$$x^{2} + 7x + 3x + 21$$</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
            <cell>$$x^{2} + 10x + 21$$</cell>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Notice that before combining like terms, we had four terms. We multiplied the two terms of the first binomial by the two terms of the second binomial—four multiplications.</p>
      <p>Be careful to distinguish between a sum and a product.</p>
      <equation>$$\begin{matrix} \textbf{Sum} &amp; &amp; &amp; \textbf{Product} \\ {x + x} &amp; &amp; &amp; {x \cdot x} \\ {2x} &amp; &amp; &amp; x^{2} \\ \text{combine like terms} &amp; &amp; &amp; \text{add exponents of like bases} \end{matrix}$$</equation>
      <example number="10.34">
        <problem>
          <p>Multiply: $$\left( {x + 6} \right)\left( {x + 8} \right).$$</p>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <table>
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <cell />
                <cell>$$\left( {x + 6} \right)\left( {x + 8} \right)$$</cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell />
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Distribute $$(x + 8)$$.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Distribute again.</cell>
                <cell>$$x^{2} + 8x + 6x + 48$$</cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Simplify.</cell>
                <cell>$$x^{2} + 14x + 48$$</cell>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </solution>
      </example>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.67">
        <p>Multiply: $$\left( {x + 8} \right)\left( {x + 9} \right).$$</p>
      </note>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.68">
        <p>Multiply: $$\left( {a + 4} \right)\left( {a + 5} \right).$$</p>
      </note>
      <p>Now we'll see how to multiply binomials where the variable has a coefficient.</p>
      <example number="10.35">
        <problem>
          <p>Multiply: $$\left( {2x + 9} \right)\left( {3x + 4} \right).$$</p>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <table>
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <cell />
                <cell>$$\left( {2x + 9} \right)\left( {3x + 4} \right)$$</cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Distribute. $$(3x + 4)$$</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Distribute again.</cell>
                <cell>$$6x^{2} + 8x + 27x + 36$$</cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Simplify.</cell>
                <cell>$$6x^{2} + 35x + 36$$</cell>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </solution>
      </example>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.69">
        <p>Multiply: $$\left( {5x + 9} \right)\left( {4x + 3} \right).$$</p>
      </note>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.70">
        <p>Multiply: $$\left( {10m + 9} \right)\left( {8m + 7} \right).$$</p>
      </note>
      <p>In the previous examples, the binomials were sums. When there are differences, we pay special attention to make sure the signs of the product are correct.</p>
      <example number="10.36">
        <problem>
          <p>Multiply: $${({4y + 3})}{({6y - 5})}.$$</p>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <table>
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <cell />
                <cell>$${({4y + 3})}{({6y - 5})}$$</cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Distribute.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Distribute again.</cell>
                <cell>$$24y^{2} - 20y + 18y - 15$$</cell>
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Simplify.</cell>
                <cell>$$24y^{2} - 2y - 15$$</cell>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </solution>
      </example>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.71">
        <p>Multiply: $${({7y + 1})}{({8y - 3})}.$$</p>
      </note>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.72">
        <p>Multiply: $$\left( {3x + 2} \right)\left( {5x - 8} \right).$$</p>
      </note>
      <p>Up to this point, the product of two binomials has been a trinomial. This is not always the case.</p>
      <example number="10.37">
        <problem>
          <p>Multiply: $$(x + 2)(x - y).$$</p>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <table>
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <cell />
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Distribute.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Distribute again.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Simplify.</cell>
                <cell>There are no like terms to combine.</cell>
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </solution>
      </example>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.73">
        <p>Multiply: $$(x + 5)(x - y).$$</p>
      </note>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.74">
        <p>Multiply: $$(x + 2y)(x - 1).$$</p>
      </note>
    </subsection>
    <subsection title="Using the FOIL Method">
      <p>Remember that when you multiply a binomial by a binomial you get four terms. Sometimes you can combine like terms to get a trinomial, but sometimes there are no like terms to combine. Let's look at the last example again and pay particular attention to how we got the four terms.</p>
      <equation>$$\left( {x + 2} \right)\left( {x - y} \right)$$</equation>
      <equation>$$x^{2} - \textit{xy} + 2x - 2y$$</equation>
      <p>Where did the first term, $$x^{2},$$ come from?</p>
      <p>It is the product of $$x\ \textit{and}\ x,$$ the first terms in $$(x + 2)\ \text{and}\ (x - y).$$</p>
      <p>The next term, $$- \textit{xy},$$ is the product of $$x\ \text{and}\ - y,$$ the two outer terms.</p>
      <p>The third term, $$+2x,$$ is the product of $$2\ \text{and}\ x,$$ the two inner terms.</p>
      <p>And the last term, $$-2y,$$ came from multiplying the two last terms.</p>
      <p>We abbreviate “First, Outer, Inner, Last” as FOIL. The letters stand for ‘First, Outer, Inner, Last’. The word FOIL is easy to remember and ensures we find all four products. We might say we use the FOIL method to multiply two binomials.</p>
      <p>Let's look at $$(x + 3)(x + 7)$$ again. Now we will work through an example where we use the FOIL pattern to multiply two binomials.</p>
      <image alt="Comparing the Distributive Property and FOIL method for multiplying binomials, like (x+3)(x+7), both resulting in x^2 + 10x + 21." />
      <example number="10.38">
        <problem>
          <p>Multiply using the FOIL method: $$\left( {x + 6} \right)\left( {x + 9} \right).$$</p>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <table>
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <cell>Step 1: Multiply the First terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Step 2: Multiply the Outer terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Step 3: Multiply the Inner terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Step 4: Multiply the Last terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Step 5: Combine like terms, when possible.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </solution>
      </example>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.75">
        <p>Multiply using the FOIL method: $$\left( {x + 7} \right)\left( {x + 8} \right).$$</p>
      </note>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.76">
        <p>Multiply using the FOIL method: $${({y + 14})}{({y + 2})}.$$</p>
      </note>
      <p>We summarize the steps of the FOIL method below. The FOIL method only applies to multiplying binomials, not other polynomials!</p>
      <note type="How To">
        <list type="ol" style="1">
          <item>Step 1. 
Multiply the First terms.</item>
          <item>Step 2. 
Multiply the Outer terms.</item>
          <item>Step 3. 
Multiply the Inner terms.</item>
          <item>Step 4. 
Multiply the Last terms.</item>
          <item>Step 5. 
Combine like terms, when possible.</item>
        </list>
      </note>
      <example number="10.39">
        <problem>
          <p>Multiply: $${({y - 8})}{({y + 6})}.$$</p>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <table>
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <cell>Step 1: Multiply the First terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Step 2: Multiply the Outer terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Step 3: Multiply the Inner terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Step 4: Multiply the Last terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Step 5: Combine like terms</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </solution>
      </example>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.77">
        <p>Multiply: $${({y - 3})}{({y + 8})}.$$</p>
      </note>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.78">
        <p>Multiply: $${({q - 4})}{({q + 5})}.$$</p>
      </note>
      <example number="10.40">
        <problem>
          <p>Multiply: $$\left( {2a + 3} \right)\left( {3a - 1} \right).$$</p>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <table>
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <cell />
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell />
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Multiply the First terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Multiply the Outer terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Multiply the Inner terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Multiply the Last terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Combine like terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </solution>
      </example>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.79">
        <p>Multiply: $$\left( {4a + 9} \right)\left( {5a - 2} \right).$$</p>
      </note>
      <note type="Try It" number="10.80">
        <p>Multiply: $$\left( {7x + 4} \right)\left( {7x - 8} \right).$$</p>
      </note>
      <example number="10.41">
        <problem>
          <p>Multiply: $$\left( {5x - y} \right)\left( {2x - 7} \right).$$</p>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <table>
            <tbody>
              <row>
                <cell />
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell />
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Multiply the First terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Multiply the Outer terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Multiply the Inner terms.</cell>
                <cell />
              </row>
              <row>
                <cell>Multiply the Last terms.</cell>
